      ":question/id": "49c1090f-0d14-5ccb-9c30-a8b9c4fffd87",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-79403",
      ":question/problem": "Express $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
