      ":question/id": "7a291781-59c8-57ab-aafe-d8f0841ec267",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-47198",
      ":question/problem": "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}\\right)=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient quotient and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
