      ":question/id": "e70f2288-3b25-5d12-a8c5-361a6e66e0f3",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-100243",
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{log}_{4}⁡(x)-{log}_{4}⁡(y) & ={log}_{4}⁡({x}^{1/3})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{x})-{log}_{4}⁡(y) \\\\  & ={log}_{4}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x}}{y}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
